<header class="mb-10 text-center"><a href="/science" class="text-sm underline">The Science</a><h1 class="mt-5 font-serif text-4xl leading-tight md:text-5xl">Trace Minerals in Electrolytes: Why French Gray Sea Salt Is More Than Just Salt</h1><p class="mt-5 text-sm">By PUREST ELECTROLYTE | Science &amp; Ingredients</p><time datetime={published} class="text-sm text-stone-500">November 9, 2026</time></header>
<div class="prose prose-stone mx-auto max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed">
<p>When most people think about electrolytes, they think about sodium and potassium. Maybe magnesium.</p><p>But what about trace minerals?</p><p>The ocean contains an extraordinary spectrum of naturally occurring minerals and elements, many present in concentrations so small they are rarely discussed.</p><p>At PUREST ELECTROLYTE, we believe hydration should start with real ingredients. That's why we use French gray sea salt harvested from the salt marshes of Guérande, France.</p><p>Our sea salt provides the major electrolytes sodium, chloride, magnesium, potassium, and calcium, along with naturally occurring trace constituents.</p><p><strong>More than just salt. A broader mineral story.</strong></p>
<figure><img src="/images/blog/pam-sweeney-salt-farmer-france.webp" alt="PUREST founder Pam Sweeney with a salt farmer in Guérande France" class="w-full rounded-lg" /><figcaption>PUREST founder Pam Sweeney with one of our salt farmers in Guérande, France.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>What Are Trace Minerals?</h2><p>Trace minerals are minerals the body requires in very small amounts, or elements that occur naturally in very low concentrations.</p><p>Some, including zinc, copper, manganese, selenium, and iodine, have well established roles in human health. They contribute to processes such as enzyme activity, thyroid function, antioxidant defense, and normal cellular function.</p><p>The ocean contains these elements and many others in an enormous range of concentrations. Scientists can identify elements in seawater at levels measured in parts per million, parts per billion, and even parts per trillion. Their presence may be extremely small, but they are still part of the ocean's natural chemistry.</p>
<h2>The Connection Between Ocean Minerals and the Human Body</h2><p>The human body is approximately 50% to 60% water, depending on age and body composition. But the fluids inside our bodies aren't simply water. They contain carefully balanced electrolytes, including sodium, chloride, potassium, magnesium, and calcium. These minerals help regulate fluid balance, nerve impulses, and muscle contractions.</p><p>The ocean also contains these electrolytes, along with a much broader spectrum of trace elements. This connection has fascinated researchers for generations.</p><p>French physiologist René Quinton explored the relationship between seawater and the body's internal environment more than a century ago. More recently, Dr. Pierre Kory has discussed Quinton's work and the diversity of trace elements in marine mineral products.</p><p>The comparison is interesting, although our bodies maintain very different mineral concentrations from seawater.</p><p>At PUREST, French gray sea salt is one way we bring naturally occurring marine minerals into our electrolyte formulation.</p>
<h2>Why French Gray Sea Salt?</h2><p>Not all salt is processed the same way. Highly refined table salt is primarily sodium chloride. French gray sea salt is traditionally harvested from coastal salt marshes, where seawater evaporates naturally. The gray color comes from contact with the mineral rich clay of the salt beds.</p><p>PUREST sources French gray sea salt from Guérande, France, a region known for its centuries old salt harvesting tradition. Our salt contains more than sodium chloride. Our supplier's laboratory analysis documents magnesium, potassium, calcium, iodine, chromium, and copper. These are naturally occurring constituents of the salt itself.</p>
<figure><img src="/images/blog/pam-sweeney-guerande-salt-marshes.webp" alt="Salt marshes and traditional salt harvesting in Guérande France" class="w-full rounded-lg" /><figcaption>Traditionally harvested French sea salt in Guérande, France.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The Minerals in PUREST's French Gray Sea Salt</h2><p>Our French gray sea salt supplier provided a Certificate of Analysis documenting the mineral composition of the ingredient.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto"><table><thead><tr><th>Mineral</th><th>Laboratory result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sodium</td><td>36.30%</td></tr><tr><td>Chloride</td><td>60.20%</td></tr><tr><td>Magnesium</td><td>0.53%</td></tr><tr><td>Calcium</td><td>0.20%</td></tr><tr><td>Potassium</td><td>0.14%</td></tr><tr><td>Iodine</td><td>Less than 0.00005%</td></tr><tr><td>Chromium</td><td>0.22 ppm</td></tr><tr><td>Copper</td><td>Less than 0.08 ppm</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><small>Source: French gray sea salt Certificate of Analysis, lot L0840215, dated April 8, 2024. These are ingredient concentrations, not quantities per serving of finished PUREST ELECTROLYTE.</small></p><p>These results establish the presence of major electrolytes and certain trace constituents in the analyzed salt. But the chemistry of the ocean extends far beyond the elements typically included on a food ingredient certificate.</p>
<h2>85 Naturally Occurring Elements in the Ocean</h2><p>The ocean is one of Earth's most complex natural chemical environments. Oceanographic research describes major minerals, minor elements, ultratrace elements, dissolved gases, and naturally occurring radioactive elements in seawater.</p><p>The following chart presents 85 elements included in broader ocean element references. Some are essential nutrients. Others are not nutrients, and some can be harmful at sufficient exposure levels.</p><p><strong>“Trace” in this chart means an element documented in the broader marine environment, without a quantified measurement in PUREST's sea salt. It does not represent a laboratory finding for our finished product.</strong></p>
