<h2>Why Electrolytes and Trace Minerals Work Together</h2><p>Electrolytes and trace minerals are not competing categories. Electrolytes are minerals that carry electrical charges when dissolved in body fluids. Sodium, potassium, chloride, magnesium, and calcium are important examples.</p><p>Essential trace minerals are needed in smaller quantities for other physiological processes. Together, these different nutrients support many of the body's normal functions.</p><p>The amount matters. A mineral can be present in nature without contributing a nutritionally meaningful amount in a particular serving. For hydration, sodium and other electrolytes remain central. Trace minerals are an additional part of the broader mineral story.</p>
<h2>Why PUREST Is More Than Just Salt</h2><p>Many hydration products start with isolated mineral ingredients. At PUREST, we start with real ingredients: freeze dried fruit and French gray sea salt.</p><p>Our sea salt naturally contains sodium and chloride, along with magnesium, potassium, calcium, and trace constituents. We believe ingredient sourcing matters. We also believe customers deserve to understand what's in the products they use every day.</p><p>That's why we share our salt's mineral analysis and <a href="/science/sea-salt-heavy-metal-test-results">heavy metal testing information</a>.</p><p><strong>Real fruit. French gray sea salt. Major electrolytes. Naturally occurring trace minerals.</strong></p><p>Nothing complicated.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Trace Minerals and Electrolytes</h2>
<h3>What are trace minerals?</h3><p>Trace minerals are minerals required by the body in very small quantities. Examples include zinc, copper, selenium, iodine, and manganese. The term is also sometimes used more broadly for elements occurring in very low environmental concentrations.</p>
<h3>Does sea salt contain trace minerals?</h3><p>Yes. Sea salt can contain naturally occurring minerals and trace elements beyond sodium chloride. The exact composition depends on the source, harvesting process, and salt sample.</p>
<h3>Does PUREST ELECTROLYTE contain trace minerals?</h3><p>PUREST uses French gray sea salt from Guérande, France. Its supplier analysis documents magnesium, calcium, potassium, iodine, chromium, and copper, in addition to sodium and chloride.</p>
<h3>What are the main electrolytes in French gray sea salt?</h3><p>Sodium and chloride are the primary electrolytes. Magnesium, potassium, and calcium are also present in smaller amounts.</p>
<h3>Are there really 85 minerals in the ocean?</h3><p>Oceanographic references describe a broad spectrum of elements in seawater, including many present in extremely low concentrations. The number depends on how elements, dissolved gases, and radioactive constituents are counted. Not all are minerals required by the body.</p>
<h3>Are natural electrolytes better than synthetic electrolytes?</h3><p>The body uses dissolved electrolyte ions regardless of whether they come from refined mineral salts or natural sources. Natural sea salt can provide a broader mixture of constituents, while the amounts of essential electrolytes remain important for hydration.</p>
<h3>What makes PUREST ELECTROLYTE different?</h3><p>PUREST combines real fruit with French gray sea salt. Rather than focusing only on sodium chloride, we highlight the naturally occurring mineral composition of our salt and make ingredient testing information available to customers.</p>
<h2>The PUREST Philosophy</h2><p>We believe the best ingredients don't need to be complicated. The ocean contains an extraordinary range of naturally occurring elements. French gray sea salt brings part of that marine mineral heritage into our hydration products.</p><p>Our bodies depend on water and electrolytes every day. For PUREST, that connection is worth respecting.</p><p><strong>MORE THAN JUST SALT.</strong></p><p><strong>REAL FRUIT. REAL SEA SALT. REAL HYDRATION.</strong></p>
